{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538800-quick-question-does-feeding-cause-unsoakable-levels-of-lethal-damage","id":1538800,"title":"Quick Question does feeding cause unsoakable levels of lethal damage?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-07-17T17:09:52-07:00","id":1538800,"title":"Quick Question does feeding cause unsoakable levels of lethal damage?","content":"So the feed maneuver draws three blood points from a target, and each blood point is considered a lethal level of damage. With that in mind you can grapple and feed in combat, and there are other creatures in the wod who can soak lethal damage like lupines, and life mages. So, if you feed from one of those targets does the blood taken out of their blood pool cause them levels of lethal damage? And if so are they able to soak that damage to keep going? Or does it work that when the ten points for mages, and the 20 for lupines are drained they instantly die? Thank you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28506-carsusbloodking","name":"Carsusbloodking","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-17T20:46:38-07:00","id":1538811,"title":"","content":"The rules aren't really that clear, but I generally say one blood point drained is one soakable level of lethal damage. Humans can't soak lethal damage so that shit is automatic. Ghouls and Werewolves get a chance to soak it, representing [insert flimsy supernatural reason here]. If you drain every blood point, they automatically die.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27252-hades","name":"Hades","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-18T00:04:26-07:00","id":1538817,"title":"","content":"In VDA20 that mechanic gets pretty out of hand if you use Serpentis 2, unless the ST houserules it.<br /> <br /> With Serpentis 2 you have an additional tongue attack which can both inflict damage and drink 3 blood points per turn. Unlike in VtM20, it's not specified that drinking is on the turn after having hit with the tongue.<br /> You also get to drink 5 BP per turn instead of 3 with your enlarged maws.<br /> Nowhere is it written that using one prevents you from using the other, so you can declare multiple actions, and attack with both. The tongue will do some damage (probably), and you will drink 8 BP (plus the Kiss, plus the automatic grapple, if the victim survives).<br /> <br /> Humans have 10 BP total. If you take out 2+ health levels with the tongue, they drop to 8- (characters have 1 less BP per health level lost). Drink 8 and they're dead.<br /> <br /> The real issue is that this works equally well on other creatures. The rules on potent blood (lower gen vamps, werewolves) are that they still house 10 BP health wise, but it counts as more for effectiveness once in the drinking vampire's body. So effectively you can still drain to death a damaged (with the tongue) werewolf in one turn. Vamps don't go so easily because they don't lose BP because of wounds, but you can still drain them in 2 turns instead of 1, assuming they have more than 80% of their blood pool full. <br /> <br /> It's pretty wild and STs should consider whether they're ok with this or they want to make some changes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-18T07:34:21-07:00","id":1538833,"title":"","content":"Unsoakable levels of lethal damage. Any living beings need blood to live. Without any blood, you die. But in truth, death occurs way before the last drop is drained.<br /> <br /> Werewolves will just keep regenerating that damage however, even while they are being drained.<br /> <br /> Mages with Life can create effects that allow them to survive without blood at all; in this case, they wouldn't really take any damage at all from losing blood","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-18T11:08:38-07:00","id":1538846,"title":"","content":"So, the general consensus seems to be that a target will only have ten blood points (barring vampires) and that it should either cause lethal or unsoakable lethal damage to a target. But that werewolves will regenerate it after the fact because they are death machines, and a life mage could do something weird that allows them to survive the blood loss, or they just die like a regular human?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28506-carsusbloodking","name":"Carsusbloodking","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-18T11:33:19-07:00","id":1538849,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Carsusbloodking</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538800-quick-question-does-feeding-cause-unsoakable-levels-of-lethal-damage?p=1538846#post1538846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, the general consensus seems to be that a target will only have ten blood points (barring vampires) and that it should either cause lethal or unsoakable lethal damage to a target. But that werewolves will regenerate it after the fact because they are death machines, and a life mage could do something weird that allows them to survive the blood loss, or they just die like a regular human?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if it's a general consensus. Personally, if a werewolf loses blood via sucking faster than his regeneration factor, at least he should go comatose (I can't remember what the name for their short coma from injuries is called) and maybe do their thing where they wake up again but suffer some major crippling wound for life... and he would still be in the vampire's maw, so liable to die for real shortly after anyway. But I'm not a werewolf expert, and have only read parts of WtA20, so maybe I missed some important detail.<br /> <br /> And at least according to VDA20 a low gen vamp still has only 10 BP for sucking purposes, even if he has more for his own use and for into how many BP they turn into once in the drinker's body.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-20T23:18:25-07:00","id":1538961,"title":"","content":"I'd think total loss of Health levels due to blood loss would kill a werewolf just like any other living animal. Before that point, supernatural regeneration should see them recovering swiftly.<br /> <br /> Mages with Life 3+ can probably do all sorts of things to either similarly recover from the effects or to alter their physiology to compensate (imagine turning themselves into plant-based life and the vampire spitting out a mouthful of useless sap). But if they haven't done it in advance I think the injury/weakness would make it much harder for them to cast such spells after the fact.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-21T08:32:10-07:00","id":1538968,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538800-quick-question-does-feeding-cause-unsoakable-levels-of-lethal-damage?p=1538961#post1538961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd think total loss of Health levels due to blood loss would kill a werewolf just like any other living animal. Before that point, supernatural regeneration should see them recovering swiftly.<br /> <br /> Mages with Life 3+ can probably do all sorts of things to either similarly recover from the effects or to alter their physiology to compensate (imagine turning themselves into plant-based life and the vampire spitting out a mouthful of useless sap). But if they haven't done it in advance I think the injury/weakness would make it much harder for them to cast such spells after the fact.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. And one has to factor in the Kiss as well, which can stop the victim from fighting back. And after that it's only 3.3 turns (at 3 BP/turn), or even less with special powers or with damage suffered, before the victim is completely dry.<br /> <br /> As per VDA20, humans (I would include mages in this) also have to make rolls not to pass out once they have lost more than a certain number of BP (I think 2). I would probably apply this to werewolves in homo form, too, and maybe to other forms after I go and re-read the details of how they work. Mind you, the rolls are Stamina based and werewolves have higher Stamina in almost all forms other than Homo, so they already have that to reflect their higher resistance, plus the faster healing rate, so it's questionable in my book that they should be given additional immunity to loss of consciousness due to blood loss.<br /> <br /> Ruling vampire bites this way has the benefit of making them a valuable tool for vamps, as they should be as one of their unique features, and making their blood drinking actually scary in a world where most other supernatural creatures can put down vampires easily.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-21T13:34:13-07:00","id":1538989,"title":"","content":"AFAIR, there's a line somewhere saying that werewolves either aren't affected by the Kiss, or have an advantage resisting it. Probably something related to expending Rage.<br /> <br /> Other than that, they can even rebound from taking damage to the last Health Box without losing conscience, I'm not convinced they should faint from blood loss.<br /> <br /> I understand what you say about making vampires scarier in some way, but at least outside Breed form, or Homid if you prefer, I think making them almost as fragile as humans detract too much from their narrative role.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-21T14:24:45-07:00","id":1538994,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538800-quick-question-does-feeding-cause-unsoakable-levels-of-lethal-damage?p=1538989#post1538989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">AFAIR, there's a line somewhere saying that werewolves either aren't affected by the Kiss, or have an advantage resisting it. Probably something related to expending Rage.<br /> <br /> Other than that, they can even rebound from taking damage to the last Health Box without losing conscience, I'm not convinced they should faint from blood loss.<br /> <br /> I understand what you say about making vampires scarier in some way, but at least outside Breed form, or Homid if you prefer, I think making them almost as fragile as humans detract too much from their narrative role.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not denying their ability to rebound from damage taken to the last Health box. If I remember correctly, if they take bashing damage to the last box, they do fall unconscious. I don't remember the details of how they regain consciousness and heal in that case.<br /> If the last box has lethal or aggravated damage, they roll to heal the damage instantly, suffer some permanent damage, and get back into the fray. But they still need to roll for this, and they don't automatically free themselves from the vampire's grip or bite.<br /> <br /> It's fair to say that they would be immune to the Kiss while frenzying. And the rules on the Kiss in VDA20 give advantages to vampires (when bitten) vs mortals, so a similar advantage could be extended to werewolves. I don't know which bonuses, beyond frenzy and specific gifts or the like, werewolves have to resist mental effects.<br /> <br /> <br /> I think at the end of the day, one should read carefully the feeding rules for the edition one is playing, and eventually check the corresponding manual for the other splat (like werewolf or mage etc) to see if there is any rule that would apply to this. Beyond this, it's houseruling territory, which anyway is always a bit the default when doing crossovers, because the manuals are not written to be compatible.<br /> <br /> But also, I don't think that what I said makes them almost as fragile as humans. The bonus to Stamina alone in some forms may suffice statistically to make a big difference between a werewolf's resistance and that of a normal human. Plus, grappling and biting a werewolf, especially if he's in a pack - as is often the case - is not easy at all, even though Potence and the rules on grappling can help a great deal because automatic successes are a huge advantage. But not every vampire has multiple dots on Potence, while every werewolf can turn into Crinos.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":". 07-21-2024, 05:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-22T09:06:50-07:00","id":1539027,"title":"","content":"Here's how i play it as ST:<br /> <br /> A vampire drinking blood from someone or something (werewolf) inflict unsoakable lethal damage (except for vampires, whose blood reserve is simply drained), because i understand werewolves's skin, muscles and bones can take bullets, claws, explosion, fire and so on but it's hard for me to imagine their blood refusing to flow down the throat of a monster. They will heal quickly anyway. For mortal there's of course no fast healing and mage can &quot;cheat&quot; with reality with vulgar magic to produce more blood or survive without blood with the Life Sphere.<br /> <br /> If mortals lose their last health box they're dead, end of story, but the vampire can suck the last blood point in their corpse to get their 10BP. If a werewolf lose his last health box he roll Rage to survivre as usual, heal 1 health per success, enter frenzy, is immune to the kiss and begin the slaughter. Mage are dead except if they used their magic before loosing their last health box to prevent their death.<br /> <br /> About the kiss, mortal can't resist except if the ST consider the character to have something special (a strong will, a trauma triggered by the fear or anger... Anything the ST consider fitting to his story as long as it's very rare). Vampires AND werewolves can resist the effect with Willpower roll or Rage if higher than Willpower for werewolf, but in that case they risk frenzying. Mages have to use their magic to protect their mind from the effect or at least get some resistance allowing them to roll their willpower. Beyond that they fall for the kiss.<br /> <br /> In fact, while I'm thinking about it, I can't remember if the book said if your can roll each turn to resist the kiss or if you have only one try... Gotta check for the next time<br /> <br /> For Werewolves blood gain, they DO NOT have 20BP in their body, each BP worth double their value, if you drink 2BP he take 2 unsoakable lethal damage and you gain 4BP total (remember that drinking werewolf blood increase difficulty for the vampire frenzy test, a valuable but dangerous meal)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 07-22-2024, 03:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-22T10:18:37-07:00","id":1539039,"title":"","content":"I run it almost exactly like lethal damage; that is that one Blood Point heals on a healthy human in one day, two BP recover in about half a week, 3 BP takes about a week to recover and can be a hazard for older or weaker humans to recover without treatment. 4 BP may require medical intervention even for healthy humans or they may have strokes, neuropathy or brain or cardiovascular damage. Too far beyond that and you better call them a paramedic and start giving them warm Gatorade.<br /> <br /> That being said, for a combat scene I kindof only count that first point against their bruised level. Beyond that it seems weird to count blood loss less than hypovolemic shock as damage in a vampire game. Hunters should be more fragile after being fed on a bit, but shouldn't really get stunned/killed by it until quite late in a combat, I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-22T11:07:40-07:00","id":1539044,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538800-quick-question-does-feeding-cause-unsoakable-levels-of-lethal-damage?p=1538961#post1538961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd think total loss of Health levels due to blood loss would kill a werewolf just like any other living animal. Before that point, supernatural regeneration should see them recovering swiftly.<br /> <br /> Mages with Life 3+ can probably do all sorts of things to either similarly recover from the effects or to alter their physiology to compensate (imagine turning themselves into plant-based life and the vampire spitting out a mouthful of useless sap). But if they haven't done it in advance I think the injury/weakness would make it much harder for them to cast such spells after the fact.</div> </div> </div> </div>The kiss paralyses and robs the victim of reason through pleasure, unless of course it is the bite of a Giovanni. A mage should be incapable of even moving much less casting a spell, the same is true of anything that goes under fangs even other vampires. If they fail the resistance roll which in V20 is self-control/instinct at difficulty 8. A mage could of course set up that counter measure before the kiss to make the vampire release them but during is another thing entirely.<br /> <br /> I am less sure on Werewolves, but they possibly get a resistance roll like vampires do.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27607-bloodyrelight","name":"BloodyRelight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27607_2.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}